      "text": "UNIVERSAL K-MATRIX FOR QUANTUM SYMMETRIC PAIRS — Let g be a symmetrizable Kac-Moody algebra and let Uq(g) de- note the corresponding quantized enveloping algebra.\n\n**UNIVERSAL K-MATRIX FOR QUANTUM SYMMETRIC PAIRS**\n\n*MARTINA BALAGOVIĆ AND STEFAN KOLB*\n\n52 pp, 42 sections.\n\n**Abstract.** Let g be a symmetrizable Kac-Moody algebra and let Uq(g) de- note the corresponding quantized enveloping algebra. In th e present paper we show that quantum symmetric pair coideal subalgebras Bc,s of Uq(g) have a universal K-matrix if g is of ﬁnite type. By a universal K-matrix for Bc,s we mean an element in a completion of Uq(g) which commutes with Bc,s and provides solutions of the reﬂection equation in all integra ble Uq(g)-modules in category O. The construction of the universal K-matrix for Bc,s bears sig- niﬁcant resemblance to the construction of the universal R- matrix for Uq(g). Most steps in the construction of the universal K-matrix are performed in the general Kac-Moody setting. In the late nineties T. tom Dieck and R. H¨ aring-Oldenburg de veloped a program of representations of categories of ribbons in a cyl inder.\n\n**Outline.** Front matter; 1 Introduction; 1.1 Background; 1.2 Universal K-matrix for coideal subalgebras; 1.3 Main results; 1.4 Organization; 2 Preliminaries on quantum groups; 2.1 The root datum; 2.2 Quantized enveloping algebras; 2.4 Lusztig's skew derivations ri and ir; 3 The completion U of Uq(g); 3.1 The algebra U; 3.2 The coproduct on U; 3.3 Quasi R-matrix and commutativity isomorphi; 4 Braided tensor categories with a cylinder tw; 4.1 Cylinder twists and the reflection equatio; 4.2 Twisted cylinder twists; 4.3 Cylinder-braided coideal subalgebras and t\n\n**Extracted claims (proposals, not verified).**\n- Theorem 5.3: ([KW92], [Kol14, Theorem 2.7]) The map\n- Theorem 5.6: ([BK15, Theorem 3.11]) Assume that conditions (i) and (ii) hold.\n- Theorem: s 2.10, 6.4] for the coideal subalgebras corresponding to the symmetric\n- Theorem 6.10: There exists a uniquely determined element X = ∑\n- Theorem 7.5: Letξ :P → K(q1/d)× be a function satisfying the recursion (7.8).\n- Theorem 9.4: The intertwiner X satisﬁes the relation",
